// bTunGsTeN Workbench 002 — SAW plan. STAGED, NOT-RUN (2026-10-07,
// result-class law: EQUIVALENCE is its own result class and has not run).
//
// Software Analysis Workbench (Galois, the tool NSA's Foundation repo
// builds its assurance methodology around) proves an implementation
// equivalent to a Cryptol specification. The spec half exists
// (../wb002-cryptol/BTungstenWB002.cry, run in CI by wb002-formal-check.sh); the
// implementation half for SAW is a RUST twin of wb002-simpleassets.mjs's
// transition oracle that does not exist yet — the JS twin is the
// executable battery's target, and SAW has no JS extraction. Named
// gaps, in order (mirrors WB001's ladder; WB002 slots after it):
//
//   1. cryptol typecheck + :check/:prove sovereignContinuity  (DONE, CI formal job)
//   2. Rust twin of sovereign/step in this repo         (later beat)
//   3. this script's equivalence + coverage proof       (last)
//
// Planned shape once (1)-(2) exist:
//
//   cryptol_load ../wb002-cryptol/BTungstenWB002.cry;
//   let stepSpec = cryptol "step";
//
//   llvm_load_bitcode "target/…/wb002_sovereign.bc";
//   let stepImpl = llvm_extract "wb002_sovereign::step";
//
//   // equivalence, all states and actions: the Rust oracle refuses
//   // exactly where the spec holds the state unchanged.
//   theorem rust_matches_spec : llvm_verify stepImpl
//     [cryptol stepSpec] [];
//
//   // with equivalence discharged, the battery's sampled histories and
//   // wrong-signer matrix become provable corollaries of
//   // sovereignContinuity rather than samples — the WB002 endgame.
//
// Run environment note: SAW release binaries are Linux x86_64; the CI
// ubuntu runner is the qualified host (tests.yml's container beat when
// it lands). Until then: UNVERIFIED, deliberately.
